    "updated_at": "2025-01-21T11:09:05Z",
    "body": "It's somewhat likely that HTML's language comes from the relevant section of CSP (https://w3c.github.io/webappsec-csp/#frame-ancestors-and-frame-options), which notes that:\n\n> In order to allow backwards-compatible deployment, the [frame-ancestors](https://w3c.github.io/webappsec-csp/#frame-ancestors) directive obsoletes the ``[X-Frame-Options](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/document-lifecycle.html#x-frame-options)`` header. If a resource is delivered with a [policy](https://w3c.github.io/webappsec-csp/#content-security-policy-object) that includes a [directive](https://w3c.github.io/webappsec-csp/#directives) named [frame-ancestors](https://w3c.github.io/webappsec-csp/#frame-ancestors) and whose [disposition](https://w3c.github.io/webappsec-csp/#policy-disposition) is \"enforce\", then the ``[X-Frame-Options](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/document-lifecycle.html#x-frame-options)`` header will be ignored, per HTML’s processing model.\n\nI meant only that XFO has no effect if a `CSP: frame-ancestors` header is present, so it's obsoleted in the sense of being overridden. Perhaps another word would have been more appropriate?\n\nThat said, I don't really find either CSP or HTML's discussion of the relationship between CSP and XFO to be harmful. I'm more concerned about MDN's current suggestion that there's some urgency in replacing existing XFO usage, which is not the case today and seems unlikely to be the case in the future. If changing HTML/CSP's language would help assuage folks' concerns in that discussion, it might be worth doing. 🤷 ",
